# GPT-6 synthetic English QA; original generated examples.
QA|What is an algorithm?|An algorithm is a finite, precise sequence of steps for solving a problem or carrying out a computation.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is an invariant in programming?|An invariant is a condition that remains true at a defined point or throughout a program operation; loop invariants are used to reason about correctness.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a precondition?|A precondition is a requirement that must hold before an operation begins for its specified behavior to be guaranteed.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a postcondition?|A postcondition is a condition guaranteed to hold after an operation completes successfully, assuming its preconditions were satisfied.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is referential transparency?|An expression is referentially transparent when it can be replaced by its value without changing program behavior, under the relevant evaluation model.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is functional programming?|Functional programming emphasizes function composition, immutable data, and limiting side effects; languages differ in how strongly they enforce these ideas.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is an API?|An application programming interface is a defined contract through which software components communicate and use one another's capabilities.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is an ABI?|An application binary interface specifies low-level conventions such as calling conventions, data layout, symbol naming, and binary compatibility between compiled components.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is serialization?|Serialization converts structured data into a representation suitable for storage or transmission; deserialization reconstructs the data from that representation.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is idempotency in an API?|An operation is idempotent when repeating it has the same intended effect as performing it once, even if the response metadata may differ.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a race condition?|A race condition occurs when a result depends on the timing or interleaving of concurrent operations in a way that can produce incorrect or inconsistent behavior.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is deadlock?|Deadlock is a state in which two or more processes or threads wait indefinitely for resources held by one another, preventing progress.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What are the four Coffman conditions?|Mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, and circular wait are the four conditions that must all hold for the classic deadlock model.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is starvation in concurrency?|Starvation occurs when a task is repeatedly denied the resources or scheduling opportunities it needs, so it makes little or no progress.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a critical section?|A critical section is a part of a program that accesses shared state and must be protected against unsafe concurrent execution.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a semaphore?|A semaphore is a synchronization primitive that maintains a counter and controls access to a limited number of resources or coordinates events.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a process?|A process is an executing program instance with its own execution state and operating-system-managed resources, including a virtual address space in typical systems.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a thread?|A thread is an execution sequence within a process; threads in one process commonly share address space and process resources.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is virtual memory?|Virtual memory gives processes an address-space abstraction and allows the operating system and hardware to map virtual addresses to physical memory or other backing storage.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a page fault?|A page fault is an event raised when a memory access requires page-table handling, such as bringing a page into memory or rejecting an invalid access.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a context switch?|A context switch saves the execution state of one task and restores another so the processor can run a different process or thread.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a system call?|A system call is a controlled interface through which a user-space program requests a service from the operating-system kernel.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a file descriptor?|A file descriptor is a small process-local integer handle used by many operating systems to refer to an open file or another I/O resource.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a symbolic link?|A symbolic link is a filesystem object that stores a path reference to another file or directory; its target may be relative or absolute.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a hard link?|A hard link is another directory entry referring to the same filesystem object or inode; it generally cannot cross filesystem boundaries and usually cannot target directories for ordinary users.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a TCP handshake?|The TCP three-way handshake uses SYN, SYN-ACK, and ACK messages to establish sequence-number state and create a connection.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is the difference between TCP and UDP?|TCP provides a reliable, ordered byte stream with connection management; UDP provides datagrams without built-in delivery or ordering guarantees.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is packet switching?|Packet switching divides data into packets that are forwarded through a network, potentially over different paths, and reassembled or processed at the destination.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is latency?|Latency is the time delay between an initiating event and its observed response; network latency is often measured as one-way delay or round-trip time.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is throughput?|Throughput is the amount of useful data or work completed per unit time; it is distinct from raw link capacity and may be reduced by overhead or contention.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a cache?|A cache stores copies of data likely to be reused so future access can be faster, trading memory capacity and consistency complexity for reduced latency.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is cache invalidation?|Cache invalidation is the process of marking cached data as stale or removing it when the underlying source changes or its validity expires.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a database transaction?|A transaction is a logical unit of database work that is committed as a whole or rolled back according to the database's transaction semantics.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What does ACID mean in databases?|ACID stands for atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability, four properties commonly used to describe reliable transaction behavior.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is database normalization?|Database normalization organizes relational schemas to reduce undesirable redundancy and update anomalies by decomposing relations according to dependencies.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a database index?|An index is an auxiliary data structure that can speed up data retrieval at the cost of storage and additional work during writes.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a primary key?|A primary key is a chosen candidate key that uniquely identifies each row in a relational table and cannot contain null values.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a foreign key?|A foreign key is a constraint that requires values in one table to correspond to a candidate or primary key in another table or the same table.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is eventual consistency?|Eventual consistency means replicas may temporarily disagree after updates but, if no new updates occur and propagation succeeds, they are expected to converge.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a distributed system?|A distributed system consists of independent computers that communicate over a network and coordinate to provide a service or solve a task.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is the CAP theorem?|The CAP theorem states that during a network partition, a distributed data system must trade off between strong consistency and availability as defined by the theorem's model.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is fault tolerance?|Fault tolerance is the ability of a system to continue providing specified service despite certain component failures.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is observability in software?|Observability is the ability to infer a system's internal state from its external outputs, commonly using logs, metrics, and traces.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a software regression?|A regression is a defect or loss of behavior introduced by a change that causes previously working functionality to fail or degrade.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is continuous integration?|Continuous integration is a development practice in which changes are integrated frequently and automatically checked with builds and tests.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is continuous delivery?|Continuous delivery keeps software in a releasable state through automated build, test, and deployment preparation; production release may still require approval.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a container image?|A container image is an immutable packaged filesystem and metadata used as the template for creating container instances.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is the difference between a container and a virtual machine?|A virtual machine typically virtualizes hardware and runs its own guest kernel; a container isolates processes while sharing the host kernel, though implementations vary.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a cryptographic nonce?|A nonce is a value intended to be used once within a specified protocol context; uniqueness or unpredictability requirements depend on the algorithm and use.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|Why should passwords be salted before hashing?|A unique random salt prevents identical passwords from producing identical stored hashes and defeats precomputed rainbow-table attacks when combined with a suitable password-hashing function.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is phishing?|Phishing is a social-engineering attack that tricks people into revealing information, installing malware, or taking an unsafe action by impersonating a trusted entity.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is the principle of least privilege?|The principle of least privilege grants each user or component only the permissions required to perform its legitimate tasks.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is defense in depth?|Defense in depth uses multiple independent or complementary security controls so that failure of one control does not automatically compromise the entire system.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is threat modeling?|Threat modeling systematically identifies assets, trust boundaries, attackers, attack paths, and mitigations during system design or review.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a vulnerability?|A vulnerability is a weakness in software, hardware, configuration, or process that can be exploited to violate a security property.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is the difference between authentication and authorization?|Authentication establishes an identity or verifies a claim; authorization determines which actions or resources that identity is allowed to access.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is public-key cryptography?|Public-key cryptography uses a mathematically related key pair, typically a public key and a private key, for operations such as encryption, signatures, or key agreement.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a digital signature?|A digital signature uses a private key to create a verifiable value over data, allowing others with the corresponding public key to check authenticity and integrity.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a certificate authority?|A certificate authority is an entity trusted to issue and sign digital certificates that bind public keys to identities under a defined policy.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is TLS certificate validation?|Certificate validation checks whether a certificate is trusted, valid for the requested identity, within its validity period, and properly chained according to the client's trust policy.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is machine learning generalization?|Generalization is a model's ability to perform well on relevant unseen examples drawn from the intended deployment distribution.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a feature in machine learning?|A feature is an input variable or representation used by a model to make predictions or decisions.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a label in supervised learning?|A label is the target value or category associated with a training example that the supervised model is intended to predict.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is cross-validation?|Cross-validation repeatedly partitions data into training and validation portions to estimate how a model or procedure may perform on unseen data.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a confusion matrix?|A confusion matrix tabulates predicted classes against actual classes, showing counts of true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives in binary classification.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is calibration in a probabilistic model?|A model is calibrated when predictions assigned a probability level correspond to approximately that empirical frequency over appropriate groups of cases.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is data drift?|Data drift is a change in the distribution of input data over time that may affect model performance or assumptions.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is concept drift?|Concept drift is a change over time in the relationship between model inputs and the target or outcome being predicted.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is an embedding?|An embedding maps an item such as a word, sentence, or image to a vector in a numerical space where useful relationships may be represented geometrically.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is cosine similarity?|Cosine similarity is the cosine of the angle between two nonzero vectors and is often used to compare embedding directions independently of vector magnitude.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is retrieval-augmented generation?|Retrieval-augmented generation retrieves relevant external information and supplies it as context to a generative model, helping ground responses in a maintained knowledge source.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a hallucination in a language model?|A hallucination is generated content presented as factual despite being unsupported, incorrect, or fabricated relative to the available evidence.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|Why is provenance important in a dataset?|Provenance records where data came from, how it was transformed, and under what license or conditions it may be used, enabling audit and correction.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a data schema?|A schema defines the structure, types, constraints, and relationships expected in a dataset or data system.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a data contract?|A data contract is an explicit agreement about data structure, semantics, quality expectations, ownership, and compatibility between producers and consumers.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a checksum?|A checksum is a value computed from data to help detect accidental changes or corruption; cryptographic hashes provide stronger properties for security-sensitive integrity checks.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is reproducible research?|Reproducible research provides sufficient data, methods, code, and environment details for others to repeat an analysis and verify its results.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a scientific control group?|A control group provides a comparison condition in an experiment, helping researchers estimate the effect of the intervention while accounting for other factors.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a confounding variable?|A confounder is a variable associated with both an exposure and an outcome that can distort an estimated relationship if not appropriately addressed.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
