QA|What is an abstract syntax tree?|An abstract syntax tree is a tree representation of program structure that omits some surface syntax details while preserving meaningful syntactic relationships.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a compiler front end?|A compiler front end typically performs lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, and construction of an intermediate representation from source code.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is an intermediate representation?|An intermediate representation is a compiler-internal form that makes program structure explicit and supports analysis and transformation before target code generation.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is static analysis?|Static analysis examines source code or intermediate representations without executing the program to identify defects, suspicious patterns, or violations of rules.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is dynamic analysis?|Dynamic analysis studies a program while it runs, using tests, instrumentation, profiling, or runtime checks to observe behavior.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a memory leak?|A memory leak occurs when allocated memory remains unavailable for reuse because the program retains it or loses the means to release it after it is no longer needed.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is use-after-free?|Use-after-free is a memory safety defect in which a program accesses an object after its storage has been released, potentially causing corruption or security vulnerabilities.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is buffer overflow?|A buffer overflow occurs when a program writes beyond the allocated bounds of a buffer, potentially corrupting adjacent memory or enabling exploitation.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is input validation?|Input validation checks that untrusted input conforms to expected type, range, format, and semantic constraints before it is used.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is output encoding in web security?|Output encoding transforms data for the context in which it is rendered so that untrusted content is treated as data rather than executable markup or script.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is cross-site scripting?|Cross-site scripting is a web vulnerability in which attacker-controlled content executes as script in another user's browser within a trusted site's origin.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is cross-site request forgery?|CSRF tricks an authenticated browser into sending an unwanted request to a site where the user has an active session; defenses include anti-CSRF tokens and appropriate cookie policies.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is content security policy?|Content Security Policy is a browser security mechanism that restricts allowed sources and behaviors for resources such as scripts, styles, and frames.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is semantic versioning?|Semantic versioning uses MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH numbers to communicate compatibility expectations, with rules for incompatible changes, backward-compatible features, and bug fixes.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is backward compatibility?|Backward compatibility means a newer version continues to support relevant behavior or interfaces expected by existing clients, within a defined compatibility scope.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is technical debt?|Technical debt is the future maintenance cost created by expedient design or implementation choices that make later changes harder or riskier.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a minimum viable product?|A minimum viable product is the smallest product version that can test a core value proposition with real users and provide useful feedback.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is an acceptance criterion?|An acceptance criterion is a specific, testable condition that a feature or work item must satisfy to be considered complete.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a user story?|A user story is a concise description of a capability from the perspective of a user or stakeholder, often accompanied by acceptance criteria.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a design trade-off?|A design trade-off is a decision in which improving one property may worsen another, such as latency versus consistency or flexibility versus simplicity.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a single point of failure?|A single point of failure is a component whose failure can stop or critically impair the whole system because no effective alternative exists.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is graceful degradation?|Graceful degradation means a system continues to provide reduced but useful functionality when some components or dependencies fail.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a service-level objective?|A service-level objective is a measurable target for a service reliability indicator over a defined time window.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is an error budget?|An error budget is the allowed unreliability implied by a service-level objective and can guide the balance between release speed and reliability work.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a canary deployment?|A canary deployment releases a change to a small portion of traffic or users first, allowing operators to observe risk before broader rollout.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is blue-green deployment?|Blue-green deployment maintains two production-like environments and switches traffic between them to reduce release downtime and simplify rollback.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
QA|What is a rollback?|A rollback restores a previously known version or configuration after a release causes unacceptable behavior; database changes may require a compatible rollback strategy.|synthetic-gpt6-en-v1
